is more flexible.  \\n- W2 and W3 are adjacent and can form chows with W1 or W4.  \\n- T5 and T6 are adjacent and can form chows with T4 or T7.  \\n\\nGiven the exposed melds: opponents have taken some tiles (e.g., W4-W5-W6 chow across, F2 pung by me, J1 pung by me).  \\nFrom game history:  \\n- Many terminals and honors have been played (F2, F4, J1, J3, T9, W9, etc.).  \\n- Tiles like T5, T6, B5, B6, B7, B8 are relatively safe as they are middle tiles and less likely to be called by opponents.  \\n\\nBest discard: B8 because it's the highest number in my hand and less connected to other tiles (only connects backward to B6-B7, but I already have B6 and B7, so B8 is redundant for chow formation). Discarding B8 keeps flexibility with W2-W3 and T5-T6 for chows, and B5-B6-B7 for a potential chow.  \\n\\nReason: Discarding B8 maintains the most tile efficiency for forming chows and pairs, while minimizing risk since middle tiles are less likely to be called by opponents given the game state.  \\n\\nAnswer: Play B8\"}"}
