es of all three suits, wind tiles and dragon tiles.\" So I need at least one tile from each of W, T, B suits, plus wind and dragon. My melds: J1 (dragon), F2 (wind), B chow (B suit), T chow (T suit). If my pair is W6, then I have W suit. If my pair is F4, then I have wind but no W suit. So only if my pair is W6 do I get \"All Types\". That is a significant difference: \"All Types\" is worth 6 points. Combined with Dragon Pung (2), Seat Wind (2), and Melded Hand (6) if I win by discard, that would total 16 points, well above 8. If I win by self-draw, I might not get Melded Hand, but I could get Self Drawn (1 point) and possibly others, but \"All Types\" alone gives 6, plus Dragon and Seat Wind gives 10, which is enough. \n\nTherefore, discarding F4 and waiting on W6 gives the potential for \"All Types\", while discarding W6 does not. So discarding F4 is superior.\n\nAlso, note that \"All Types\" requires the hand to contain tiles of all three suits. With pair W6, I have W, T, B. With pair F4, I lack W suit. So indeed, only with W6 pair.\n\nThus, I should discard F4 to keep W6 as my pair, aiming to win with W6 to score \"All Types\".\n\nReason: Discarding F4 allows my hand to include all three suits (W, T, B) when I win with W6, qualifying for the \"All Types\" pattern worth 6 points. Combined with Dragon Pung (2 points) and Seat Wind (2 points), and potentially Melded Hand (6 points) if I win by discard, I can easily exceed the 8-point minimum. Discarding W6 would not give \"All Types\" because I would lack the W suit, reducing scoring potential.\n\nAnswer: Play F4"}
